            "evidence_validated": true,
            "valid": true,
            "eval": true
        },
        {
            "source_entity_instance": "GRISLI2.0",
            "source_entity_type": "Source",
            "relation": "USES_PHYSICAL_SCHEME",
            "target_entity_instance": "linear basal friction law",
            "target_entity_type": "PhysicalScheme",
            "paragraph": "For temperate-based grounded ice, we impose a linear basal friction law as follow (Brondex et al., 2017):\n\u0442\u044c = -\u0432\u0438\u0432",
            "ontology_conformed": false,
            "evidence_validated": true,
            "valid": true,
            "eval": true
        },
        {
            "source_entity_instance": "GRISLI2.0",
            "source_entity_type": "Source",
            "relation": "HAS_PHYSICAL_FEATURE",
            "target_entity_instance": "Cartesian grid",
            "target_entity_type": "PhysicalFeature",
            "paragraph": "In this work, we used the GRISLI2.0 (hereafter, GRISLI) ice-sheet model fully described in Quiquet et al. (2018). The model is run on a Cartesian grid (20 km \u00d7 20 km) to simulate the BKIS retreat from 21 to 10 ka. GRISLI has demonstrated its ability to reproduce the grounding line migration of the Antarctic ice sheet over several glacial-interglacial cycles (Quiquet et al., 2018; Ritz et al., 2001) and has been employed in several international intercomparison exercises for shorter time scales, including both Greenland and Antarctica (e.g., Goelzer et al., 2020; Seroussi et al., 2020). Recently, it was also used to simulate the sensitivity of the Eurasian ice sheet to variations in atmospheric and oceanic temperatures (van Aalderen et al., 2024).",
            "ontology_conformed": true,
            "evidence_validated": true,
            "valid": true,
            "eval": true
        },
        {
            "source_entity_instance": "GRISLI2.0",
            "source_entity_type": "Source",
            "relation": "HAS_SOURCE_COMPONENT",
            "target_entity_instance": "elastic lithosphere-relaxed asthenosphere model",
            "target_entity_type": "SourceComponent",
            "paragraph": "The GRISLI model also accounts for isostatic adjustment, represented by an elastic lithosphere-relaxed asthenosphere model (Le Meur and Huybrechts, 1996).\nThe representation of glacio-isostatic adjustment (GIA) in our simulations could also bring some uncertainties. In GRISLI, bedrock deformation is computed using an Elastic Lithosphere-Relaxed Asthenosphere (ELRA) model with a relaxation time of 3 kyr. However, regional studies suggest",
            "ontology_conformed": true,
            "evidence_validated": false,
            "valid": true,
            "eval": true
        },
        {
            "source_entity_instance": "GRISLI2.0",
            "source_entity_type": "Source",
            "relation": "USES_PHYSICAL_SCHEME",
            "target_entity_instance": "Darcy's law for subglacial hydrology",
            "target_entity_type": "PhysicalScheme",
            "paragraph": "where to is the basal shear stress, ub is the basal ice velocity and \u1e9e is the basal drag coefficient. In our model, \u1e9e depends on the effective pressure N and on bed conditions and is expressed as \u1e9e = Cf. Csedim. N, where Cf is an internal constant parameter. The coefficient Csedim accounts for the presence of subglacial sediments and takes values of 0.1 or 1 depending on the sediment thickness (Laske, 1997). N is governed by the subglacial hydrology, which is computed according to Darcy's law (Quiquet et al., 2018). We fixed a lower bound of the basal drag coefficient, \u1e9emin=10 Pa.s.m\u00af\u00b9, to prevent excessively fast ice flow and ensures the preservation of grounded ice in warm-based areas. For ice shelves, basal friction is neglected.",
            "ontology_conformed": false,
            "evidence_validated": true,
            "valid": true,
            "eval": true
        },
        {
            "source_entity_instance": "GRISLI2.0",
            "source_entity_type": "Source",
            "relation": "HAS_PHYSICAL_FEATURE",
            "target_entity_instance": "20 km x 20 km grid",
            "target_entity_type": "PhysicalFeature",
            "paragraph": "In this work, we used the GRISLI2.0 (hereafter, GRISLI) ice-sheet model fully described in Quiquet et al. (2018). The model is run on a Cartesian grid (20 km \u00d7 20 km) to simulate the BKIS retreat from 21 to 10 ka. GRISLI has demonstrated its ability to reproduce the grounding line migration of the Antarctic ice sheet over several glacial-interglacial cycles (Quiquet et al., 2018; Ritz et al., 2001) and has been employed in several international intercomparison exercises for shorter time scales, including both Greenland and Antarctica (e.g., Goelzer et al., 2020; Seroussi et al., 2020). Recently, it was also used to simulate the sensitivity of the Eurasian ice sheet to variations in atmospheric and oceanic temperatures (van Aalderen et al., 2024).\nHowever, the relatively coarse resolution of GRISLI (20 km \u00d7 20 km) may limit the representation of small confined ice shelves, potentially leading to an underestimation of the ocean's impact. Higher-resolution simulations would allow a more accurate assessment of the formation of such confined ice shelves and help determine whether, if present, they significantly influence BKIS dynamics.",
            "ontology_conformed": true,
            "evidence_validated": false,
            "valid": true,
            "eval": true
        },
        {
            "source_entity_instance": "GRISLI2.0",
            "source_entity_type": "Source",
            "relation": "USES_PHYSICAL_SCHEME",
            "target_entity_instance": "Pollard and DeConto (2012) sub-shelf melt rate parameterization",
            "target_entity_type": "PhysicalScheme",
            "paragraph": "Following the approach of Pollard and DeConto (2012), the sub-shelf melt rate (OM) is determined based on ocean temperature and salinity using the formula:\nOM = KtpwCw |To - Tf|(To - Tf)\nPiLf",
            "ontology_conformed": true,
            "evidence_validated": true,
            "valid": true,
            "eval": true
        },
        {
            "source_entity_instance": "GRISLI2.0",
            "source_entity_type": "Source",
            "relation": "USES_PHYSICAL_SCHEME",
            "target_entity_instance": "ice flux at the grounding line as a function of the ice thickness to the power of five",
            "target_entity_type": "PhysicalScheme",
            "paragraph": "The GRISLI model used in this study is based on the same parameter settings and parametrizations as those of van Aalderen et al., 2024 (see also Tables S1 and S2), inlcuding the prescribed ice flux at the grounding line, expressed as a function of the ice thickness to the power of five, following Tsai et al. (2015).",
            "ontology_conformed": false,
            "evidence_validated": true,
            "valid": true,
            "eval": true
        },
        {
            "source_entity_instance": "GRISLI2.0",
            "source_entity_type": "Source",
            "relation": "USES_PHYSICAL_SCHEME",
            "target_entity_instance": "thickness threshold calving",
            "target_entity_type": "PhysicalScheme",
            "paragraph": "Calving is based on a thickness threshold of 250 m below which ice is calved. The GRISLI model also accounts for isostatic adjustment, represented by an elastic lithosphere-relaxed asthenosphere model (Le Meur and Huybrechts, 1996).",
            "ontology_conformed": false,
            "evidence_validated": true,
            "valid": true,
